AWS/Data Engineering file-processing scenarios

What is a loop? A loop repeats the same code for many files or records, so you don't write it once per item.
for loop: It takes one item at a time from a collection and stores it in a temporary variable.
How the loop works: Each pass (iteration) assigns the next item to the loop variable until the items run out.
Data Engineering example: Looping over a list of file names to print "Processing: file" is how you'd handle multiple S3 objects.
Loop through numbers: A for loop can go through any list of numbers and use each value in turn.
range(): It repeats something a set number of times, starting at 0 and stopping before the given number.
range(start, stop): You choose the starting number, and the loop stops just before the stop value.
range(start, stop, step): The step sets how much the value increases on each pass, such as 2 for every second number.
Process files with file number: range() can generate file numbers (1 to 5) to simulate processing a numbered series of files.
Loop with if condition: Combining for with if (like endswith(".csv")) lets you process only the items that match.
Loop through dictionary: Looping directly over a dictionary gives you its keys.
Dictionary keys and values: .items() gives you both the key and the value on each pass, which is useful for JSON-like data.
List of dictionaries: Looping over a list of dictionaries lets you read fields like customer["name"] from each record, combining Day 3 and Day 4.
while loop: It keeps running as long as its condition stays true.
Why increment matters: If the counter never changes (count += 1), the condition stays true forever and you get an infinite loop.
Retry example: A while loop with an attempt counter is the basic idea behind retrying a failed API call or pipeline.
break: It immediately stops the entire loop when a condition is met.
break in Data Engineering: The loop ends as soon as it sees a "STOP" item, so later files are never processed.
continue: It skips only the current item and moves on to the next one.
continue in Data Engineering: It skips unwanted files, such as .txt files, while still processing the rest.
break vs continue: break stops the whole loop, while continue skips just one item.
Nested loop: A loop inside another loop runs the inner loop fully for each item of the outer loop.
AWS nested loop example: Nested loops let you go through every combination of folders and dates, like partitions in S3.
Real scenario: Loop through a list of file dictionaries and use continue to skip failed files while processing successful ones.
Count successful files: Start a counter at 0 and add 1 each time a file succeeds, giving a total at the end.
